PROPOSED CHANGE OUTSIDE THIS BRIEF'S WRITE LIST -- NOT APPLIED. (pri 126 phase 7, item B)

TARGET  tools/land.py   (strategy owns tools/; a solver may not edit it, so it is proposed as a diff)
VERIFIED  applies clean with a STRICT `git apply` on the landed tree (checked in a scratch worktree;
          nothing in the repository was modified and nothing was deleted). The patched file parses.

WHAT IT DOES.  Wires the sealed modern holdout into the landing routine so it is read ONCE PER LANDING:
  * `sealed_board_read(repo, record)` runs `experiments/exp_sealed_modern_holdout_v1.py --board` with
    OMP/OPENBLAS/MKL_NUM_THREADS=2 and PYTHONHASHSEED=0, and stamps the current git HEAD onto the landing
    line the cell appends to data/exp_sealed_modern_holdout_v1/board_landings.jsonl.
  * THE READ COUNTER REFUSES A SECOND READ AT THE SAME HEAD: before running, it scans the jsonl for a
    record whose `head_sha` equals `git rev-parse HEAD`; if one exists it prints a refusal and REUSES that
    record. Re-running tools/land.py therefore cannot spend the seal twice on one tree.
  * `sealed_ledger_fragment()` puts the sealed numbers INTO the INTEGRATION_LEDGER row -- each row with its
    own recomputed floor and an explicit "CI-sep" / "NOT CI-sep" verdict, plus the document count and the
    state-twin scorer version.
  * A failed sealed read RAISES: a landing is not recorded against an unmeasured seal.
  * Skipped on `--dry-run` and in TEMP MODE (neither may spend the seal), and skippable per record with
    `"skip_sealed_read": true` for a landing that provably does not touch the reader's path.

ONE DEVIATION FROM THE INSTRUCTION, STATED.  The instruction was "after the record is written and before
the commit". Those two cannot both hold if the sealed numbers are to appear IN the ledger row, because the
ledger row is built by `build_changes()` BEFORE any file is written. The hook therefore runs immediately
BEFORE `build_changes()` -- still strictly before the commit, and before any doc file is written. If the
preference is genuinely "after the writes", move the call below `run_changes` and drop the ledger fragment;
the sealed numbers then live only in board_landings.jsonl.

RISK OF THIS RECOMMENDATION, STATED.  It adds ~4 CPU-minutes to every landing on a contended laptop (279
reader calls; measured 265s of loop time, much longer in wall time under load), and it makes `tools/land.py`
fail when the instrument fails -- by design, but it is a new way for a landing to stop. The `skip_sealed_read`
escape hatch exists so that a documentation-only landing is not blocked; using it for a reader-path landing
would silently spend nothing and should be treated as a defect.

NOT VERIFIED BY A RUN.  The diff applies and the patched file parses; `tools/land.py` was NOT executed with
the hook (running it would write to notes/ and commit, which is strategy's lane, not a solver's).

